window.PHYS={"unit":{"unit":2,"title":"Nerves, Senses and Muscle","chapters":"Sherwood Chapters 5 to 8","course":"BIO 141 Human Physiology","teacher":"Dr. Khan Quintana","app":"Physiology Practice","key":"physio.practice.u2.v1","order_blurb":"NMJ, cross bridges, reflex arcs and more","exam":{"name":"Exam 2","date":"2026-10-20","label":"Tue Oct 20"}},"groups":[["5","Chapter 5 · The nervous system"],["6","Chapter 6 · Sensory physiology"],["7","Chapter 7 · Autonomic nervous system and the NMJ"],["8","Chapter 8 · Skeletal muscle"]],"topics":[{"id":"kc1","ch":"5","title":"Who makes myelin","core":"Myelin insulates axons so action potentials jump and travel fast. In the brain and spinal cord (CNS) oligodendrocytes make it, and one oligodendrocyte wraps segments of many axons. In the peripheral nerves (PNS) Schwann cells make it, and one Schwann cell wraps one segment of one axon.","trap":"Learning \"Schwann cell = myelin\" without the CNS/PNS split. Always find the location word first: brain or cord means oligodendrocyte.","q":"MS and Guillain-Barré both strip myelin, but from different cells. Which cell is the target in each?","a":"CNS (brain, cord): oligodendrocytes, one cell wraps many axons. PNS (nerves): Schwann cells, one cell wraps one segment of one axon. MS attacks CNS myelin; Guillain-Barré attacks PNS myelin."},{"id":"kc2","ch":"5","title":"Astrocytes and the blood-brain barrier","core":"Astrocytes are the most common glial cell in the CNS. Their foot processes wrap the brain capillaries and make the capillary cells seal together with tight junctions. That seal is the blood-brain barrier: fat-soluble molecules (O₂, CO₂, alcohol) cross it, most water-soluble drugs do not.","trap":"Picking the ependymal cell because it \"handles brain fluid.\" Ependymal cells line the ventricles and help make CSF; astrocytes keep the capillary barrier.","q":"A drug works in the body but never reaches the brain. What barrier stopped it, which cells build it, and what does get across?","a":"Astrocytes: their foot processes induce tight junctions in brain capillaries. Lipid-soluble molecules (O₂, CO₂, alcohol) pass; most water-soluble drugs are kept out."},{"id":"kc3","ch":"5","title":"Gray and white matter: the inversion","core":"Gray matter is cell bodies, dendrites and synapses, where the computing happens. White matter is myelinated axon tracts, the cables. In the brain the gray is outside (the cortex) and the white is deep. In the spinal cord it flips: gray is the inner butterfly, white is the outer shell.","trap":"Assuming gray matter is always on the outside. That is only true of the brain.","q":"Where is gray matter in the brain? In the spinal cord?","a":"Brain: gray outside (cortex), white deep. Spinal cord: gray inside (butterfly), white outside (the tracts)."},{"id":"kc4","ch":"5","title":"The brain stem: the part you cannot lose","core":"The medulla and pons hold the vital centers for breathing rhythm, heart rate and blood pressure. A small lesion there can kill, while a much larger area of cortex can be lost without threatening life. That is why brain death testing checks brain stem function.","trap":"Calling the cortex \"the most important part\" because it thinks. You can survive cortical damage; you cannot survive losing the centers that keep you breathing.","q":"Why is a small brain stem lesion more dangerous than a large cortical one?","a":"The medulla and pons hold the centers for breathing rhythm, heart rate and blood pressure. Lose them and the body cannot keep itself breathing or circulating."},{"id":"kc5","ch":"5","title":"CSF: the fluid cushion","core":"From outside in, the brain is protected by bone, three meninges (dura, arachnoid, pia), cerebrospinal fluid and the blood-brain barrier. CSF is made by the choroid plexus in the ventricles, fills the ventricles and the subarachnoid space, and drains into venous blood at the arachnoid villi. Its main protective job is to cushion the brain against blows.","trap":"Thinking CSF is a second bloodstream that feeds neurons. It carries a little waste and nutrient, but its main protective job is mechanical cushioning.","q":"Name the brain's protective layers from the outside in. What is CSF's main protective job?","a":"Skull, dura mater, arachnoid mater, CSF in the subarachnoid space, pia mater, then the blood-brain barrier. CSF is a shock absorber that cushions the brain."},{"id":"kc6","ch":"5","title":"The homunculus: a map drawn by sensitivity","core":"The somatosensory cortex (parietal lobe, just behind the central sulcus) holds a map of the body. The map is distorted: hands, lips and tongue are huge, the trunk and legs are small. Cortex area follows receptor density, not body size.","trap":"Reasoning that big body parts get more cortex. Fingertips and lips are tiny but densely innervated, so they dominate the map.","q":"Why do the lips and fingertips take up so much more of the sensory map than the whole back does?","a":"How densely packed its sensory receptors are, not how big the part is. Fingertips and lips get the most."},{"id":"kc7","ch":"5","title":"The cerebellum: the movement editor","core":"The cerebellum compares the movement you intended with the movement actually happening, using feedback from muscles and joints, and corrects it in real time. Damage gives overshoot, wobble on the way to a target (intention tremor), a wide unsteady walk and poor coordination, with normal strength and normal ability to start the movement.","trap":"Mixing it up with the basal nuclei. Cerebellum problems show up DURING movement; basal nuclei problems show up AT REST and at the START of movement.","q":"What does the cerebellum do, and what does damage look like?","a":"It compares intended with actual movement and corrects errors. Damage: overshoot and wobble during movement (intention tremor), unsteady gait, normal strength."},{"id":"kc8","ch":"5","title":"The basal nuclei and Parkinson's disease","core":"The basal nuclei (caudate, putamen, globus pallidus, with the substantia nigra) do two jobs: block unwanted movement at rest and help start wanted movement. In Parkinson's disease, dopamine neurons from the substantia nigra die, so both jobs fail: tremor at rest and trouble starting a movement.","trap":"Calling a resting tremor cerebellar. A tremor that is worst at rest and fades with purposeful movement points to the basal nuclei.","q":"What two jobs do the basal nuclei do, and what happens in Parkinson's?","a":"Suppress unwanted movement at rest and help start wanted movement. In Parkinson's, dopamine neurons of the substantia nigra die: resting tremor plus difficulty starting movement."},{"id":"kc9","ch":"5","title":"The hippocampus and consolidation","core":"Consolidation moves information from short-term memory into long-term storage, and the hippocampus is essential for it. After hippocampal damage, short-term memory still works and old long-term memories are intact, but no new long-term memories form.","trap":"Choosing \"short-term memory\" or \"retrieval.\" If the story says short-term memory works and old memories are fine, the only step left to fail is consolidation.","q":"What does the hippocampus do, and what does damage look like?","a":"It consolidates short-term memories into long-term storage. Damage: short-term memory and old memories intact, but no new long-term memories."},{"id":"kc10","ch":"5","title":"The thalamus: the relay station","core":"Sensory signals stop at the thalamus, which sorts them and sends each to the right area of cortex for conscious perception. Smell is the exception: it reaches emotion and memory areas without stopping there first. The hypothalamus, just below, is different: it runs homeostasis (temperature, hunger, thirst).","trap":"Mixing up thalamus and hypothalamus because the names sound alike. Thalamus relays sensation; hypothalamus runs temperature, hunger and thirst.","q":"What does the thalamus do? How is the hypothalamus different?","a":"Thalamus: relays sensory signals to the cortex (all senses except smell). Hypothalamus: homeostasis, such as temperature, hunger and thirst."},{"id":"kc11","ch":"5","title":"Dorsal root in, ventral root out","core":"Each spinal nerve joins the cord by two roots. The dorsal (back) root carries afferent, sensory signals into the cord; the cell bodies of those sensory neurons sit in the dorsal root ganglion. The ventral (front) root carries efferent, motor signals out. Cut the dorsal root: sensation lost, movement kept. Cut the ventral root: movement lost, sensation kept.","trap":"Linking \"dorsal\" with back muscles and movement. Dorsal tells you where the root enters, and it is purely sensory.","q":"Which root of a spinal nerve brings information into the cord, and which one sends commands out?","a":"Dorsal root: afferent (sensory) fibers into the cord, cell bodies in the dorsal root ganglion. Ventral root: efferent (motor) fibers out of the cord."},{"id":"kc12","ch":"5","title":"The cord as cable and computer","core":"The spinal cord is a cable (white matter tracts to and from the brain) and a computer (reflex circuits in the gray matter). In the withdrawal reflex, the arc closes in the cord: receptor, afferent neuron, interneuron, efferent motor neuron, muscle. Your hand moves before the signal reaches the brain, so the brain finds out afterward.","trap":"Believing every decision needs the brain. The reflex loop is finished in the cord; the brain is not in the loop.","q":"Name the five parts of a reflex arc. Where does the withdrawal reflex close?","a":"Receptor, afferent (sensory) neuron, integrating center (interneuron in the cord's gray matter), efferent (motor) neuron, effector (muscle). It closes in the spinal cord, before the brain feels pain."},{"id":"kc13","ch":"6","title":"Frequency coding: how intensity is signaled","core":"Every action potential is the same size (all-or-none), so a stronger stimulus cannot make a bigger spike. Instead it makes a bigger receptor potential, which fires more action potentials per second (frequency), and it usually wakes more receptors at once (recruitment).","trap":"Picturing a stronger signal as a taller spike. Stronger means more spikes per second, not bigger spikes.","q":"A light touch and a firm press land on the same receptor. What is different about the signal its neuron sends?","a":"More action potentials per second (frequency coding), plus more receptors recruited. Each spike stays the same size because it is all-or-none."},{"id":"kc14","ch":"6","title":"The hair cell: bending becomes a signal","core":"Hair cell stereocilia stand in rows from short to tall, each tied to its taller neighbor by a tip link. Bending toward the tallest stretches the tip links and pulls open mechanically gated cation channels; K⁺ from the endolymph rushes in and the cell depolarizes (a graded receptor potential). It releases more transmitter, and the afferent neuron fires faster. Bending away slackens the links: channels close and the cell hyperpolarizes.","trap":"Saying the hair cell fires action potentials. It makes graded receptor potentials; the afferent neuron fires the action potentials.","q":"What happens when stereocilia bend toward the tallest one? Away from it?","a":"Toward: tip links stretch, cation (K⁺) channels open, the hair cell depolarizes (graded) and releases more transmitter. Away: links slacken, channels close, it hyperpolarizes."},{"id":"kc15","ch":"7","title":"The autonomic transmitter-receptor table","core":"All preganglionic fibers, in both divisions, release acetylcholine onto nicotinic receptors at the ganglion. Parasympathetic postganglionic fibers release acetylcholine onto muscarinic receptors. Most sympathetic postganglionic fibers release norepinephrine onto adrenergic (alpha and beta) receptors.","trap":"Squeezing it into \"ACh = parasympathetic, NE = sympathetic.\" Both divisions use ACh at the ganglion; they differ only at the target organ.","q":"Fill the autonomic table: preganglionic, parasympathetic postganglionic, sympathetic postganglionic.","a":"Preganglionic (both): ACh on nicotinic. Parasympathetic postganglionic: ACh on muscarinic. Sympathetic postganglionic (most): NE on adrenergic. Exception: sympathetic to sweat glands uses ACh on muscarinic."},{"id":"kc16","ch":"7","title":"The sweat gland exception","core":"Sympathetic fibers to eccrine sweat glands release acetylcholine onto muscarinic receptors. They are sympathetic by anatomy (thoracolumbar origin, short preganglionic, long postganglionic, active in fight or flight) but cholinergic by chemistry. That is why Botox and atropine both reduce sweating.","trap":"Applying the general sympathetic rule (NE on adrenergic) when the question names the sweat gland.","q":"Which transmitter and receptor do sympathetic fibers to sweat glands use?","a":"Acetylcholine on muscarinic receptors. Anatomically sympathetic, chemically cholinergic."},{"id":"kc17","ch":"7","title":"Dual innervation: two opposite instructions","core":"Most organs get both divisions with opposite effects. Sympathetic (fight or flight): heart rate up, airways and pupils dilate, digestion slows, saliva small and thick, bladder wall relaxes. Parasympathetic (rest and digest): heart rate down through the vagus, airways and pupils constrict, digestion speeds, saliva copious and watery, bladder wall contracts.","trap":"Thinking an organ has only one autonomic input. Block one division and the other one's effect takes over.","q":"Sympathetic vs parasympathetic: heart, airways, pupil, gut, saliva, bladder?","a":"Sympathetic: HR up, airways dilate, pupils dilate, gut slows, small thick saliva, bladder relaxes. Parasympathetic: HR down, airways constrict, pupils constrict, gut speeds, copious watery saliva, bladder contracts."},{"id":"kc18","ch":"7","title":"The NMJ: calcium triggers acetylcholine release","core":"An action potential arrives at the motor axon terminal, voltage-gated Ca²⁺ channels open, Ca²⁺ enters, and SNARE proteins fuse vesicles with the membrane. ACh crosses the cleft, binds nicotinic receptors on the end plate, and makes an end-plate potential that fires a muscle action potential. Acetylcholinesterase then breaks ACh down to end the signal.","trap":"Saying Na⁺ triggers release. Na⁺ carries the action potential to the terminal; Ca²⁺ is the release trigger.","q":"Walk the NMJ from the action potential to the muscle action potential.","a":"AP at terminal, voltage-gated Ca²⁺ channels open, Ca²⁺ enters, vesicles fuse (SNAREs), ACh released, ACh binds nicotinic receptors, end-plate potential, muscle AP. Acetylcholinesterase ends it."},{"id":"kc19","ch":"7","title":"The end-plate potential's safety margin","core":"The end-plate potential is graded, not an action potential, yet it fires a muscle action potential every time: it is far bigger than threshold needs. So one action potential in a motor neuron gives one action potential in each muscle fiber it supplies. Myasthenia gravis destroys receptors and shrinks that margin until some end-plate potentials fail.","trap":"Calling the EPP an action potential because it is reliable. Reliable is not the same as all-or-none.","q":"Is the end-plate potential an action potential? Why does it never fail?","a":"No, it is graded. It is so much larger than threshold (a big safety margin) that it always triggers a muscle action potential."},{"id":"kc20","ch":"8","title":"The withdrawal reflex","core":"A painful stimulus makes cord interneurons excite the flexors of that limb (pull away) and inhibit its extensors (get out of the way). Inhibiting the opposing muscle is reciprocal inhibition. Without it the two groups would fight and the limb would freeze.","trap":"Writing \"excite the flexors\" and stopping. Half the answer is inhibiting the extensors.","q":"Your hand jerks back from a hot pan. Inside that arm, which muscles are excited and which are switched off?","a":"Flexors are excited and contract; extensors are inhibited and relax (reciprocal inhibition), so the limb can pull away."},{"id":"kc21","ch":"8","title":"The crossed extensor reflex","core":"When one foot withdraws from a tack, interneurons crossing the cord excite the extensors of the opposite leg (and inhibit its flexors). That leg stiffens and takes your whole weight, so you stay standing.","trap":"Assuming a reflex mirrors itself. If both legs flexed, you would fall.","q":"One foot jerks up off something sharp. What keeps you from falling over at that moment?","a":"Its extensors contract and its flexors relax, so it stiffens and supports your weight while the injured foot lifts."},{"id":"kc22","ch":"8","title":"Muscle spindle vs Golgi tendon organ","core":"Muscle spindles lie parallel to the muscle fibers and detect length (stretch); they drive the monosynaptic stretch reflex, like the knee jerk. Golgi tendon organs sit in series, in the tendon, and detect tension (force); high tension triggers a polysynaptic inhibitory reflex that relaxes the muscle.","trap":"Picking the Golgi tendon organ for the knee jerk because the hammer hits the tendon. The tap stretches the muscle, and the spindle senses length.","q":"Muscle spindle vs Golgi tendon organ: where is each, and what does each detect?","a":"Spindle: parallel to fibers, detects LENGTH, monosynaptic stretch reflex (knee jerk). Golgi tendon organ: in series in the tendon, detects TENSION, inhibitory reflex."},{"id":"kc23","ch":"8","title":"Excitation-contraction coupling","core":"The muscle action potential runs along the sarcolemma and down the T tubules. Voltage sensors there (DHP receptors) open the Ca²⁺ release channels (ryanodine receptors) of the sarcoplasmic reticulum, and Ca²⁺ floods the cytosol. Relaxation: the SERCA pump uses ATP to put Ca²⁺ back into the SR.","trap":"Saying the Ca²⁺ comes in from outside the cell. In skeletal muscle it comes from the internal SR store.","q":"Where does the Ca²⁺ for skeletal muscle contraction come from, and how does it get out?","a":"From the sarcoplasmic reticulum, released when the AP travels down the T tubules. It goes back into the SR by an ATP-using pump (SERCA), which lets the muscle relax."},{"id":"kc24","ch":"8","title":"Troponin, tropomyosin and the switch","core":"At rest, tropomyosin lies over the myosin binding sites on actin, held there by troponin. Ca²⁺ binds troponin, troponin shifts tropomyosin, the binding sites open, and cross bridges can form.","trap":"Reversing the order. Calcium binds tropoNIN first, then tropoMYOSIN moves out of the way.","q":"What does Ca²⁺ bind, and what happens next?","a":"Ca²⁺ binds troponin, troponin moves tropomyosin off the binding sites on actin, and myosin heads can attach."},{"id":"kc25","ch":"8","title":"The cross-bridge cycle","core":"Starting attached: (1) ATP binds myosin and the head lets go of actin; (2) myosin splits ATP and the head re-cocks, keeping ADP and Pi; (3) the cocked head binds actin if Ca²⁺ has opened the sites; (4) Pi release triggers the power stroke, pulling the thin filament inward, then ADP leaves; (5) it repeats while Ca²⁺ and ATP last.","trap":"Thinking ATP is only for the power stroke. ATP binding is what detaches the head; with no ATP the heads stay stuck (rigor mortis).","q":"Name the five steps of the cross-bridge cycle, starting attached.","a":"1 ATP binds, head detaches. 2 ATP split, head re-cocks. 3 Head binds actin. 4 Pi release, power stroke (then ADP leaves). 5 Repeat while Ca²⁺ and ATP are present."},{"id":"kc26","ch":"8","title":"Sliding filaments","core":"In contraction, thin filaments slide inward over thick filaments; neither filament shortens. Z lines move closer. The A band (the length of the thick filaments) stays the same; the I band and H zone narrow.","trap":"Saying the A band narrows. It never changes, because the thick filament never shortens.","q":"During contraction, which bands narrow and which stays the same?","a":"I band and H zone narrow; Z lines move closer. The A band stays the same width because the thick filaments do not shorten."},{"id":"kc27","ch":"8","title":"Isometric vs isotonic","core":"The name tells you what stays the same. Isometric (\"same length\"): tension rises but the muscle does not change length, like holding a plank. Isotonic (\"same tension\"): tension stays constant while the muscle changes length and the load moves, shortening (concentric) or lengthening under load (eccentric).","trap":"Swapping them: isometric is constant LENGTH, not constant tension.","q":"Isometric vs isotonic: what stays the same in each?","a":"Isometric: length stays the same, tension rises, nothing moves. Isotonic: tension stays the same, the muscle shortens or lengthens, the load moves."},{"id":"kc28","ch":"8","title":"Length-tension","core":"A muscle makes its maximum tension at its optimal length, where thick and thin filaments overlap so the most cross bridges can form. Stretched too far, overlap is small and fewer bridges form. Too short, thin filaments overlap each other and thick filaments hit the Z lines. Either way, less tension, even with normal nerves and plenty of ATP.","trap":"Looking for a nerve or fuel explanation. Length-tension is geometry: overlap sets how many cross bridges can form.","q":"Pull a sarcomere much longer than its best length and it gets weaker. What has gone wrong between the filaments?","a":"Thick and thin filaments barely overlap, so fewer cross bridges can form. It is geometry, not nerves or ATP."},{"id":"kc29","ch":"8","title":"Muscle fiber types","core":"Slow-oxidative (Type I) fibers have many mitochondria, capillaries and myoglobin (red, \"dark meat\"); they make ATP aerobically and resist fatigue but make less force. Fast-glycolytic (Type IIx) fibers have few mitochondria, rely on glycolysis, make more force and fatigue quickly. Fast-oxidative (Type IIa) sit in between.","trap":"Thinking slow fibers resist fatigue because they are stronger. They are weaker per contraction; aerobic metabolism is what lets them keep going.","q":"Slow-oxidative vs fast-glycolytic: fuel, fatigue, force, example?","a":"Type I slow-oxidative: aerobic, many mitochondria, fatigue resistant, less force, posture and endurance. Type IIx fast-glycolytic: glycolysis, few mitochondria, fatigue fast, more force, sprinting and lifting."}],"mcq":[{"kc":"kc1","q":"Multiple sclerosis is an autoimmune attack on the myelin of the brain and spinal cord. Whose myelin is being destroyed?","c":["Schwann cells","Oligodendrocytes","Microglia","Ependymal cells"],"a":1,"why":"MS strikes CNS myelin, and in the CNS myelin is made by oligodendrocytes.","no":["Schwann cells myelinate peripheral nerves, which MS spares.","","Microglia are the CNS immune cells. They may join the attack, but they make no myelin.","Ependymal cells line the ventricles and help make CSF."],"id":"q1"},{"kc":"kc1","q":"Guillain-Barré syndrome strips myelin from peripheral nerves, such as the nerves to the legs. Which glial cell made that myelin?","c":["Oligodendrocyte","Astrocyte","Schwann cell","Satellite cell"],"a":2,"why":"Peripheral nerves are PNS, and PNS myelin comes from Schwann cells.","no":["Oligodendrocytes myelinate only inside the brain and spinal cord.","Astrocytes are CNS support cells that keep the blood-brain barrier.","","Satellite cells surround cell bodies in ganglia; they do not wrap axons in myelin."],"id":"q2"},{"kc":"kc1","q":"Losing one oligodendrocyte can slow conduction in more axons than losing one Schwann cell. Why?","c":["Oligodendrocytes make a thicker myelin layer","One oligodendrocyte wraps segments of many axons; one Schwann cell wraps one segment of one axon","Schwann cells can be replaced by astrocytes","Oligodendrocytes also produce the action potential"],"a":1,"why":"One oligodendrocyte sends processes to many axons, so its loss bares many segments at once. A Schwann cell covers only one segment of one axon.","no":["Thickness is not the difference that matters here; the number of axons each cell serves is.","","Astrocytes do not replace Schwann cells, and Schwann cells live in the PNS.","Neurons generate action potentials; glia only insulate and support."],"id":"q3"},{"kc":"kc2","q":"Alcohol reaches the brain within minutes, but many water-soluble antibiotics barely enter it. What explains the difference?","c":["Alcohol is lipid-soluble, so it passes through brain capillary cells; water-soluble drugs are blocked by the tight junctions","Brain capillaries have large pores that only alcohol fits through","Ependymal cells pump alcohol into the brain","Alcohol is carried across in the cerebrospinal fluid"],"a":0,"why":"Brain capillary cells are sealed by tight junctions (induced by astrocytes), so a molecule must go through the cells. Lipid-soluble molecules like alcohol, O₂ and CO₂ dissolve through the membranes; most water-soluble drugs cannot.","no":["","Brain capillaries are the opposite of leaky: they lack the pores of other capillaries.","Ependymal cells line the ventricles; they do not pump drugs into brain tissue.","CSF cushions the brain; it is not the route alcohol takes from blood to neurons."],"id":"q4"},{"kc":"kc2","q":"Compared with capillaries in a muscle, the capillary cells in the brain are joined by:","c":["Gap junctions that let ions pass freely","Wide pores for fast exchange","Tight junctions, induced by astrocyte foot processes","Myelin wrapped by oligodendrocytes"],"a":2,"why":"Astrocyte foot processes signal brain capillary cells to form tight junctions. That seal is the blood-brain barrier.","no":["Gap junctions connect cells electrically; they are not what seals brain capillaries.","Pores belong to leakier capillaries elsewhere in the body.","","Myelin wraps axons, not capillaries."],"id":"q5"},{"kc":"kc2","q":"A classmate says, \"The ependymal cell forms the blood-brain barrier, because it handles brain fluid.\" What is the best correction?","c":["Correct: ependymal cells seal the capillaries","Ependymal cells line the ventricles and help make CSF; astrocytes maintain the capillary barrier","Microglia form the barrier by eating anything that crosses","There is no barrier; drugs fail because neurons destroy them"],"a":1,"why":"Two different cells with two different fluid jobs: ependymal cells line the ventricles and help make CSF, and astrocytes keep the capillary barrier.","no":["That is exactly the trap: \"fluid\" does not mean \"barrier.\"","","Microglia are immune defenders, not the barrier.","The barrier is real, and it is why many drugs never reach neurons."],"id":"q6"},{"kc":"kc3","q":"A cross-section of the spinal cord shows a butterfly-shaped core. What is that core made of?","c":["White matter: myelinated tracts","Gray matter: cell bodies, dendrites and synapses","Cerebrospinal fluid","The dorsal root ganglia"],"a":1,"why":"In the cord the gray matter is the inner butterfly, where interneurons and motor neuron cell bodies compute.","no":["White matter is the outer shell of the cord.","","CSF is only in the small central canal and around the cord.","Dorsal root ganglia sit outside the cord on the dorsal roots."],"id":"q7"},{"kc":"kc3","q":"Why does white matter look white?","c":["It is packed with neuron cell bodies","Its axons are wrapped in fatty myelin","It is full of cerebrospinal fluid","It has no blood supply"],"a":1,"why":"Myelin is mostly lipid, and that fat gives the tracts their white color.","no":["Cell bodies are what make gray matter gray.","","CSF is in the ventricles and subarachnoid space, not the tracts.","White matter has a blood supply; color comes from myelin."],"id":"q8"},{"kc":"kc3","q":"Long tracts carry touch and pain up the spinal cord to the brain. Where in the cord do they run?","c":["In the inner gray butterfly","In the outer white matter","In the central canal","In the ventral root"],"a":1,"why":"Tracts are bundles of myelinated axons, which is white matter. In the cord the white matter is outside.","no":["The gray butterfly holds cell bodies and synapses, not the long cables.","","The central canal holds CSF.","The ventral root carries motor axons out to the body, not up to the brain."],"id":"q9"},{"kc":"kc4","q":"After a head injury, a patient's cortex is badly damaged, yet she breathes on her own and keeps a steady heart rhythm. Which region must still be working?","c":["The cerebral cortex","The medulla of the brain stem","The hippocampus","The cerebellum"],"a":1,"why":"Breathing rhythm, heart rate and blood pressure are run by centers in the medulla and pons.","no":["The story says the cortex is badly damaged, and she still breathes.","","The hippocampus consolidates memory; it does not run breathing.","The cerebellum edits movement; it does not keep you breathing."],"id":"q10"},{"kc":"kc4","q":"Doctors test brain stem reflexes and the drive to breathe when they decide whether brain death has occurred. Why the brain stem?","c":["It is the largest part of the brain","Its centers keep breathing and circulation going; without them the body cannot sustain itself","It stores long-term memories","It makes all of the cerebrospinal fluid"],"a":1,"why":"If the brain stem is gone, the body cannot keep its own breathing and circulation going.","no":["The cerebrum is far larger than the brain stem.","","Memory storage is in the cortex, with the hippocampus consolidating.","CSF is made by the choroid plexus in the ventricles."],"id":"q11"},{"kc":"kc4","q":"Which set of centers sits in the medulla?","c":["Memory, language and planning","Breathing rhythm, heart rate and blood pressure","Temperature, hunger and thirst","Smoothing and correcting movement"],"a":1,"why":"The medulla holds the vital centers: breathing rhythm, heart rate and blood pressure.","no":["Those are cortical jobs.","","That is the hypothalamus.","That is the cerebellum."],"id":"q12"},{"kc":"kc5","q":"Where does cerebrospinal fluid surround the brain?","c":["Between the skull and the dura mater","In the subarachnoid space, between the arachnoid and the pia","Inside the myelin sheaths","Inside the capillaries of the blood-brain barrier"],"a":1,"why":"CSF fills the ventricles and the subarachnoid space, which lies between the arachnoid mater and the pia mater.","no":["Above the dura is the epidural region; CSF is deeper.","","Myelin is tightly wrapped membrane with no fluid space for CSF.","Capillaries hold blood, not CSF."],"id":"q13"},{"kc":"kc5","q":"Which structure makes most of the cerebrospinal fluid?","c":["The choroid plexus in the ventricles","Astrocyte foot processes","The dura mater","The arachnoid villi"],"a":0,"why":"The choroid plexus in the ventricles makes CSF (about 500 mL a day). It drains back into venous blood at the arachnoid villi.","no":["","Astrocytes keep the blood-brain barrier; they do not secrete CSF.","The dura is a tough protective membrane, not a fluid factory.","The arachnoid villi are where CSF drains OUT, not where it is made."],"id":"q14"},{"kc":"kc5","q":"From the skull inward, which order of the meninges is correct?","c":["Pia, arachnoid, dura","Dura, arachnoid, pia","Arachnoid, dura, pia","Dura, pia, arachnoid"],"a":1,"why":"Dura mater (tough, outer), arachnoid mater (web-like, middle), pia mater (delicate, hugging the brain). CSF lies between arachnoid and pia.","no":["That is the order from the brain outward.","","The dura is always the outermost layer.","The arachnoid sits between dura and pia."],"id":"q15"},{"kc":"kc6","q":"Your back has far more skin than your fingertip. Which gets more somatosensory cortex, and why?","c":["The back, because it has more surface area","The fingertip, because its receptors are packed far more densely","They get equal cortex","The back, because it has more muscles"],"a":1,"why":"Cortex area follows receptor density. A fingertip is tiny but crowded with receptors, so it gets a big share.","no":["That is the body-size trap; size does not set cortex area.","","The homunculus is famously unequal.","Muscles do not set the size of the sensory map."],"id":"q16"},{"kc":"kc6","q":"Where is the primary somatosensory cortex?","c":["Frontal lobe, just in front of the central sulcus","Parietal lobe, just behind the central sulcus","Occipital lobe, at the back of the brain","Temporal lobe, near the ear"],"a":1,"why":"The somatosensory strip sits in the parietal lobe right behind the central sulcus. Motor cortex sits right in front of it.","no":["That is the primary motor cortex.","","The occipital lobe is vision.","The temporal lobe handles hearing."],"id":"q17"},{"kc":"kc6","q":"The motor cortex also has a distorted body map, with huge areas for the hands and mouth. What does that size reflect?","c":["How large those muscles are","How finely those parts must be controlled","How many bones those parts contain","How close those parts are to the brain"],"a":1,"why":"Like the sensory map, the motor map follows precision: hands and mouth need very fine control, so they get more cortex.","no":["Thigh muscles are huge but get a small area.","","Bone count has nothing to do with it.","Distance from the brain does not shape the map."],"id":"q18"},{"kc":"kc7","q":"A roadside sobriety test asks a driver to touch their nose with eyes closed and walk heel to toe. Alcohol makes those tasks fail mainly by impairing:","c":["The basal nuclei","The cerebellum","The thalamus","The hippocampus"],"a":1,"why":"Those tests check coordination and correction during movement, the cerebellum's job, which alcohol impairs.","no":["The basal nuclei start movements and quiet resting movement; the tests check coordination during movement.","","The thalamus relays sensation to the cortex.","The hippocampus consolidates memory."],"id":"q19"},{"kc":"kc7","q":"A tremor that is absent at rest but appears as the hand closes in on a target points to damage in:","c":["The basal nuclei","The cerebellum","The medulla","The dorsal root"],"a":1,"why":"A tremor during movement, worst near the target, is an intention tremor: the cerebellum is failing to correct.","no":["A basal nuclei tremor is worst at rest and fades with movement, the opposite timing.","","The medulla runs vital centers, not movement correction.","The dorsal root carries sensation into the cord."],"id":"q20"},{"kc":"kc7","q":"To correct a movement, the cerebellum must know where the limb actually is. Which input supplies that?","c":["Proprioceptors in muscles and joints, such as muscle spindles","Hair cells of the cochlea","The hypothalamus","Cerebrospinal fluid pressure"],"a":0,"why":"The cerebellum compares the plan from the motor cortex with feedback from proprioceptors about the limb's real position.","no":["","Cochlear hair cells serve hearing.","The hypothalamus runs homeostasis.","CSF pressure does not report limb position."],"id":"q21"},{"kc":"kc8","q":"Parkinson's disease is caused by the death of neurons that send which neurotransmitter to the basal nuclei?","c":["Acetylcholine","Dopamine","Norepinephrine","GABA"],"a":1,"why":"Dopamine neurons of the substantia nigra die, and the basal nuclei lose both jobs: quieting resting movement and starting wanted movement.","no":["Acetylcholine is the NMJ and autonomic transmitter; its loss is not what causes Parkinson's.","","Norepinephrine is the main sympathetic transmitter.","GABA is used inside the basal nuclei circuit, but the neurons that die in Parkinson's release dopamine."],"id":"q22"},{"kc":"kc8","q":"Dopamine itself cannot cross the blood-brain barrier, but L-dopa can, and brain cells turn it into dopamine. Why does L-dopa help Parkinson's?","c":["It replaces the missing dopamine in the basal nuclei","It blocks dopamine receptors","It strengthens cerebellar correction","It increases acetylcholine at the NMJ"],"a":0,"why":"Parkinson's is a dopamine shortage, so a dopamine precursor that crosses the barrier refills the supply. Notice the blood-brain barrier at work here, too.","no":["","Blocking dopamine would make Parkinson's worse.","The cerebellum is not the problem in Parkinson's.","The NMJ is working normally in Parkinson's."],"id":"q23"},{"kc":"kc8","q":"Huntington's disease destroys neurons of the basal nuclei so the brake on unwanted movement fails. Which sign fits?","c":["Involuntary, jerky, dance-like movements (chorea)","Complete flaccid paralysis","Loss of all touch sensation","Overshoot only when reaching for a target"],"a":0,"why":"One basal nuclei job is suppressing unwanted movement. Lose the brake and unwanted movements break through.","no":["","Paralysis comes from motor neuron or NMJ failure, not basal nuclei loss.","Touch is relayed by the thalamus to the parietal cortex.","Overshoot while reaching is cerebellar."],"id":"q24"},{"kc":"kc9","q":"In 1953 a patient known as H.M. had both hippocampi removed. Afterward he could hold a conversation and remembered his childhood, but forgot a new person within an hour. What failed?","c":["Short-term memory","Retrieval of old memories","Consolidation into long-term memory","Sensory input"],"a":2,"why":"He could hold things briefly (short-term works) and recall his childhood (old memories work). The step that failed was moving new material into long-term storage.","no":["A conversation needs short-term memory, and he could hold one.","He retrieved old childhood memories just fine.","","His senses worked; he saw and heard the new person."],"id":"q25"},{"kc":"kc9","q":"Sleep and spaced practice before an exam help you remember the material for weeks. Which process are they strengthening?","c":["Consolidation","Frequency coding","Reciprocal inhibition","Sensory adaptation"],"a":0,"why":"Moving material from short-term into long-term storage is consolidation, the process that needs the hippocampus.","no":["","Frequency coding is how neurons signal intensity.","Reciprocal inhibition relaxes an opposing muscle in a reflex.","Adaptation is a receptor fading with a steady stimulus."],"id":"q26"},{"kc":"kc9","q":"Which statement about short-term and long-term memory is correct?","c":["Short-term memory is large and permanent","Short-term memory holds a little for seconds to hours; long-term memory is large and lasts years","Long-term memory forms without the hippocampus","Both are stored only in the hippocampus"],"a":1,"why":"Short-term memory is small and temporary; long-term memory is large and durable. The hippocampus moves material from the first to the second.","no":["That describes long-term memory.","","New long-term memories need the hippocampus for consolidation.","The hippocampus consolidates; long-term memories end up stored across the cortex."],"id":"q27"},{"kc":"kc10","q":"Which sense reaches emotion and memory areas WITHOUT stopping at the thalamus first?","c":["Vision","Hearing","Touch","Smell"],"a":3,"why":"Smell is the exception to the thalamic relay, one reason smells trigger strong memories and emotions.","no":["Visual signals relay in the thalamus on the way to the occipital cortex.","Auditory signals relay in the thalamus on the way to the temporal cortex.","Touch relays in the thalamus on the way to the somatosensory cortex.",""],"id":"q28"},{"kc":"kc10","q":"A small stroke damages the hypothalamus. Which problem is most likely?","c":["Body temperature, hunger and thirst become poorly regulated","Touch can no longer reach the cortex","Reaching movements overshoot","Breathing rhythm stops"],"a":0,"why":"The hypothalamus runs homeostasis: temperature, hunger, thirst and links to the pituitary.","no":["","That is the thalamus, the name-alike trap.","Overshoot is cerebellar.","Breathing rhythm is in the medulla."],"id":"q29"},{"kc":"kc10","q":"A lesion destroys the thalamic relay for body sensation. The skin receptors, nerves and spinal cord all work. What happens?","c":["Everything is normal, because the receptors work","Conscious feeling from the body is lost, but spinal reflexes still work","Spinal reflexes are lost, but feeling is normal","The patient feels everything as pain"],"a":1,"why":"Without the relay, signals never reach the cortex, so there is no conscious sensation. Reflexes close in the cord and do not need the thalamus.","no":["Working receptors are not enough; the signal must reach the cortex.","","Backwards: reflexes live in the cord, feeling needs the relay.","Destroying the relay removes sensation rather than amplifying it."],"id":"q30"},{"kc":"kc11","q":"The chickenpox virus hides for years in the cell bodies of sensory neurons, then can return as shingles. Where are those cell bodies?","c":["In the ventral horn of the gray matter","In the dorsal root ganglion","In the ventral root","In the white matter tracts"],"a":1,"why":"Sensory (afferent) neurons have their cell bodies in the dorsal root ganglion, just outside the cord.","no":["The ventral horn holds motor neuron cell bodies.","","The ventral root carries motor axons, not sensory cell bodies.","White matter is axon tracts, not cell bodies."],"id":"q31"},{"kc":"kc11","q":"Polio destroys motor neuron cell bodies in the ventral horn of the cord. Which result fits?","c":["Lost sensation, normal movement","Weak or paralyzed muscles, normal sensation","Lost sensation and lost movement equally","Loss of the blood-brain barrier"],"a":1,"why":"Motor neurons send their axons out the ventral root to muscles. Kill them and the muscles lose their command; the sensory side is untouched.","no":["Sensation runs through the dorsal root, which polio spares.","","Only the motor side is hit.","The blood-brain barrier is unrelated."],"id":"q32"},{"kc":"kc11","q":"A signal travels from the spinal cord out to a muscle. What is it called, and which root does it use?","c":["Afferent, dorsal root","Efferent, ventral root","Afferent, ventral root","Efferent, dorsal root"],"a":1,"why":"Efferent means traveling away from the CNS (commands). Commands leave by the ventral root.","no":["Afferent means toward the CNS.","","Afferent signals enter by the dorsal root, not the ventral.","The dorsal root is purely sensory."],"id":"q33"},{"kc":"kc12","q":"Months after a complete spinal cord injury in the neck, a nurse pinches the patient's toe and the leg pulls back, but the patient feels nothing. Why?","c":["The brain still controls the leg directly","The reflex circuit is in the lower cord, below the injury, but the cable to the brain is cut","The muscle detected the pinch by itself","The thalamus is damaged"],"a":1,"why":"The withdrawal arc closes in the lower cord, so it still works. The ascending tracts are cut, so the signal never reaches the brain to be felt.","no":["The cut cord blocks commands from the brain to the leg.","","Muscles do not sense pinches; receptors and neurons do.","The thalamus is fine; the signal never gets that far."],"id":"q34"},{"kc":"kc12","q":"In the withdrawal reflex arc, which neuron lies entirely inside the gray matter of the cord?","c":["The afferent (sensory) neuron","The interneuron","The efferent (motor) neuron","The postganglionic neuron"],"a":1,"why":"Interneurons are the integrating center: they live in the gray matter and connect the incoming sensory neuron to outgoing motor neurons.","no":["The sensory neuron starts at a skin receptor and has its cell body in the dorsal root ganglion.","","The motor neuron's axon leaves through the ventral root to reach the muscle.","Postganglionic neurons are autonomic, not part of this somatic reflex."],"id":"q35"},{"kc":"kc12","q":"Why is a withdrawal reflex faster than deciding to move your hand?","c":["Reflex signals travel at a higher voltage","The path is short with few synapses, and it does not travel up to the brain and back","Reflexes skip the muscle","Reflexes use larger action potentials"],"a":1,"why":"A reflex closes in the cord through a few synapses. A decision needs the signal to go up to the brain, get processed, and come back down.","no":["Action potentials are all-or-none; voltage does not change with urgency.","","The muscle is the effector; without it nothing moves.","Action potentials are the same size in every case."],"id":"q36"},{"kc":"kc13","q":"Which change does NOT happen when a touch on your fingertip gets stronger?","c":["The receptor potential gets larger","More action potentials fire per second","More receptors are activated","Each action potential gets taller"],"a":3,"why":"Action potentials are all-or-none, so they never get taller. The graded receptor potential does grow, and that drives faster firing and recruitment.","no":["The receptor potential is graded, so it does grow with stimulus strength.","That is frequency coding, which does happen.","That is recruitment, which does happen.",""],"id":"q37"},{"kc":"kc13","q":"A touch neuron fires 5 action potentials per second for a light touch. The touch becomes a firm press. Which recording is most likely?","c":["5 per second, each twice as tall","60 per second, each the same height","60 per second, each twice as tall","5 per second, conducting more slowly"],"a":1,"why":"Firing rate rises; spike height does not change.","no":["Spike height cannot change: all-or-none.","","The rate is right, but height still cannot change.","Conduction speed is set by axon diameter and myelin, not stimulus strength."],"id":"q38"},{"kc":"kc13","q":"Why can't a neuron signal a stronger stimulus by making a bigger action potential?","c":["Once threshold is reached, the voltage-gated channels open fully and every spike reaches the same peak","Stronger stimuli block Na⁺ channels","The myelin limits the height","Bigger spikes would travel backward"],"a":0,"why":"Threshold sets off the full, self-reinforcing opening of voltage-gated Na⁺ channels, so each action potential runs its complete course to the same peak.","no":["","A stronger stimulus opens more channels on the receptor, not fewer.","Myelin changes speed, not height.","The refractory period, not spike size, keeps signals going one way."],"id":"q39"},{"kc":"kc14","q":"A hair bundle is pushed toward its SHORTEST stereocilia. What does the hair cell do?","c":["Tip links stretch, channels open, the cell depolarizes","Tip links go slack, channels close, the cell hyperpolarizes and releases less transmitter","The hair cell fires an action potential","Nothing; only bending toward the tallest has an effect"],"a":1,"why":"Bending away slackens the tip links, the few channels open at rest close, and the cell hyperpolarizes.","no":["That is bending TOWARD the tallest.","","Hair cells make graded receptor potentials, not action potentials.","Bending both ways changes the signal; that is how the cell codes direction."],"id":"q40"},{"kc":"kc14","q":"In hearing, which cell actually fires action potentials toward the brain?","c":["The hair cell","The afferent neuron of the auditory nerve","The tectorial membrane","The supporting cell"],"a":1,"why":"The hair cell makes a graded receptor potential and releases transmitter; the afferent neuron it synapses on fires the action potentials.","no":["The hair cell is a receptor cell with graded potentials only.","","The tectorial membrane is a structure the hairs press against, not a cell.","Supporting cells hold hair cells in place."],"id":"q41"},{"kc":"kc14","q":"Hair cell channels open because:","c":["Tip links physically pull them open when the stereocilia bend","Voltage-gated Na⁺ channels reach threshold","A neurotransmitter binds to them","Light strikes the cell"],"a":0,"why":"They are mechanically gated channels. The pull of a stretched tip link opens them.","no":["","That describes an axon firing, not a hair cell transducing.","Hair cells release transmitter; their transduction channels are opened by force.","Light is the stimulus for photoreceptors."],"id":"q42"},{"kc":"kc15","q":"Hexamethonium blocks nicotinic receptors at autonomic ganglia. What does it do?","c":["Blocks only sympathetic output","Blocks only parasympathetic output","Blocks output from BOTH divisions","Blocks only skeletal muscle"],"a":2,"why":"Every preganglionic fiber, in both divisions, uses ACh on nicotinic receptors at the ganglion. Block those and both divisions go quiet.","no":["The sympathetic ganglia use the same receptor as the parasympathetic ones.","The parasympathetic ganglia use the same receptor as the sympathetic ones.","","Its target is the ganglionic nicotinic receptor."],"id":"q43"},{"kc":"kc15","q":"A sympathetic postganglionic fiber to the heart releases ____ onto ____ receptors.","c":["ACh, muscarinic","ACh, nicotinic","Norepinephrine, adrenergic (beta-1)","Norepinephrine, nicotinic"],"a":2,"why":"Most sympathetic postganglionic fibers release norepinephrine onto adrenergic receptors; on the heart those are beta-1.","no":["That is the parasympathetic postganglionic pairing.","That is the preganglionic pairing at the ganglion.","","Norepinephrine acts on adrenergic receptors, not nicotinic ones."],"id":"q44"},{"kc":"kc15","q":"Beta blockers block adrenergic receptors on the heart. Which division's effect on the heart do they reduce?","c":["Sympathetic","Parasympathetic","Both equally","Neither; they act on skeletal muscle"],"a":0,"why":"Adrenergic receptors receive norepinephrine from sympathetic postganglionic fibers, so blocking them blunts the sympathetic speed-up.","no":["","Parasympathetic fibers act on muscarinic receptors.","Only the adrenergic side is blocked.","Skeletal muscle uses nicotinic receptors."],"id":"q45"},{"kc":"kc15","q":"Where are muscarinic receptors found?","c":["On every autonomic ganglion neuron","On target organs of parasympathetic postganglionic fibers, and on sweat glands","On skeletal muscle end plates","Only in the brain"],"a":1,"why":"Parasympathetic postganglionic fibers release ACh onto muscarinic receptors at their targets, and the sympathetic fibers to sweat glands do too.","no":["Ganglion neurons have nicotinic receptors.","","End plates have nicotinic receptors.","Muscarinic receptors are all over the body's parasympathetic targets."],"id":"q46"},{"kc":"kc16","q":"Botox injections are used to treat heavy underarm sweating. Botox blocks the release of ACh. Why does it work?","c":["Sweat glands are run by parasympathetic fibers","The sympathetic fibers to sweat glands release ACh, not norepinephrine","Botox blocks adrenergic receptors","Botox destroys the sweat glands"],"a":1,"why":"Sweat glands are the exception: their sympathetic fibers are cholinergic. Stop ACh release and the glands get no signal.","no":["Sweating is sympathetic.","","Botox stops ACh from being released; it does not touch adrenergic receptors.","The glands survive; they just are not stimulated."],"id":"q47"},{"kc":"kc16","q":"A patient on high-dose atropine (a muscarinic blocker) gets hot and dry on a summer day. Why does he sweat less?","c":["Atropine blocks norepinephrine at sweat glands","Atropine blocks the muscarinic receptors that sympathetic ACh acts on at sweat glands","Atropine boosts parasympathetic sweating","Atropine blocks nicotinic receptors at the ganglia"],"a":1,"why":"Sympathetic sweat-gland fibers release ACh onto muscarinic receptors, so a muscarinic blocker shuts sweating down.","no":["Sweat glands do not use norepinephrine; that is the exception.","","Atropine blocks muscarinic receptors, so it cannot boost anything that uses them.","Atropine is muscarinic, not nicotinic."],"id":"q48"},{"kc":"kc16","q":"If sweat gland fibers release ACh, why are they still called sympathetic?","c":["They come from the thoracolumbar cord, have short preganglionic and long postganglionic fibers, and fire in fight or flight","They come from the brain stem and sacral cord","Any fiber that releases ACh is sympathetic","They synapse directly on the gland with no ganglion"],"a":0,"why":"The name follows anatomy and behavior, not the chemical. Anatomically sympathetic, chemically cholinergic.","no":["","Brain stem and sacral origin is parasympathetic.","Parasympathetic fibers release ACh too.","Every autonomic pathway has a ganglion between two neurons."],"id":"q49"},{"kc":"kc17","q":"Why is it often hard to urinate when you are very nervous?","c":["Nervousness boosts parasympathetic activity, which locks the bladder shut","Sympathetic activity relaxes the bladder wall, favoring storage","The bladder has no autonomic nerves","Nervousness blocks the dorsal roots"],"a":1,"why":"Fight or flight relaxes the bladder wall so urine is stored. Emptying needs the parasympathetic division to contract it.","no":["Nervousness is sympathetic, and parasympathetic activity would contract the bladder and help emptying.","","The bladder has dual innervation.","The dorsal roots carry sensation and are not blocked by nerves."],"id":"q50"},{"kc":"kc17","q":"A heart transplant leaves the new heart with no nerve connections. Its resting rate is often about 90 to 110 beats per minute. Why faster than normal?","c":["The new heart is more sympathetic","The vagal (parasympathetic) brake is gone","The heart now uses norepinephrine as its pacemaker","Transplanted hearts have more muscarinic receptors"],"a":1,"why":"The pacemaker sets its own rate near 100. Normally the vagus slows it to about 70; with no nerves, that brake is missing.","no":["With no nerves it gets no sympathetic input either.","","Pacemaker cells start each beat on their own.","Without the vagus there is no ACh to act on the receptors anyway."],"id":"q51"},{"kc":"kc17","q":"Right before a speech your mouth feels dry. Why?","c":["Parasympathetic activity makes thick saliva","Sympathetic dominance gives only a small, thick secretion, and the watery parasympathetic flow drops","The salivary glands have no nerve supply","Saliva is blocked by the blood-brain barrier"],"a":1,"why":"Rest and digest makes copious watery saliva; fight or flight makes a small, thick amount.","no":["Parasympathetic saliva is copious and watery.","","Salivary glands get both divisions.","The blood-brain barrier has nothing to do with saliva."],"id":"q52"},{"kc":"kc17","q":"Many asthma inhalers open the airways by mimicking which division's effect on the bronchioles?","c":["Sympathetic (beta-2 receptors relax airway muscle)","Parasympathetic (muscarinic receptors)","Somatic motor","Neither; they act on the diaphragm"],"a":0,"why":"Sympathetic stimulation dilates the airways through beta-2 receptors, and inhalers activate beta-2.","no":["","Parasympathetic stimulation constricts the airways.","Somatic motor neurons run skeletal muscle, not airway smooth muscle.","The target is the bronchiole smooth muscle."],"id":"q53"},{"kc":"kc18","q":"Botulinum toxin destroys the SNARE proteins in motor axon terminals. What happens?","c":["ACh floods the cleft and muscles spasm","Vesicles cannot fuse, ACh is not released, and muscles go limp","Nicotinic receptors are permanently blocked","Ca²⁺ cannot leave the sarcoplasmic reticulum"],"a":1,"why":"SNAREs fuse vesicles with the membrane when Ca²⁺ arrives. Without them no ACh is released: flaccid paralysis.","no":["Spasm would be too much ACh; botulinum gives too little.","","Botulinum acts before the receptor, at release.","The problem is upstream, at the nerve terminal."],"id":"q54"},{"kc":"kc18","q":"In Lambert-Eaton syndrome, antibodies block voltage-gated Ca²⁺ channels at motor axon terminals. Which step fails first?","c":["The action potential reaching the terminal","Ca²⁺ entering the terminal, so less ACh is released","ACh binding to nicotinic receptors","The power stroke"],"a":1,"why":"Ca²⁺ entry is the release trigger. Fewer working Ca²⁺ channels means less ACh per impulse, and weakness.","no":["Na⁺ channels carry the action potential to the terminal, and they work.","","The receptors are fine; there is just less ACh.","The muscle machinery is fine; it is not being told to contract."],"id":"q55"},{"kc":"kc18","q":"Curare, an arrow poison, binds the nicotinic receptors on the end plate without opening them. What happens?","c":["ACh is released but cannot open the receptors, so there is no end-plate potential and the muscle stays limp","ACh release stops","Muscles lock in spasm","Ca²⁺ floods the terminal"],"a":0,"why":"Release is normal, but the receptors are occupied by curare, so no end-plate potential forms.","no":["","Release is upstream and unaffected.","Blocking receptors gives limpness, not spasm.","Curare acts on the muscle side, not the nerve terminal."],"id":"q56"},{"kc":"kc18","q":"At the NMJ, Na⁺ and Ca²⁺ do different jobs. Which pairing is right?","c":["Na⁺ triggers vesicle release; Ca²⁺ carries the action potential","Na⁺ carries the action potential down the axon; Ca²⁺ entering the terminal triggers ACh release","Both trigger release equally","Neither is involved; ACh diffuses out on its own"],"a":1,"why":"Two ions, two jobs, two places: Na⁺ for the action potential along the axon, Ca²⁺ for release at the terminal.","no":["Exactly backwards.","","Only Ca²⁺ triggers release.","Release needs Ca²⁺ entry and vesicle fusion."],"id":"q57"},{"kc":"kc19","q":"In myasthenia gravis, antibodies destroy many end-plate receptors. Why do patients get weaker with repeated use, like drooping eyelids by evening?","c":["The end-plate potential shrinks; its safety margin is gone, so with repeated firing some fall below threshold","The end-plate potential becomes an action potential","Ca²⁺ can no longer enter the terminal","Too much ACh is released"],"a":0,"why":"Fewer receptors mean a smaller end-plate potential. Normally the margin is huge; in myasthenia it is thin, and repeated firing tips some below threshold.","no":["","The EPP is always graded, never an action potential.","That is Lambert-Eaton, a different disease.","The problem is too few receptors, not too much ACh."],"id":"q58"},{"kc":"kc19","q":"The end-plate potential is a graded potential. Which property shows that?","c":["It is always exactly the same size","Its size depends on how much ACh binds, and it fades with distance instead of propagating","It travels down the T tubules on its own","It has an absolute refractory period"],"a":1,"why":"Graded potentials vary in size and decay with distance. The EPP depends on the amount of ACh bound and spreads only locally to trigger the muscle action potential.","no":["Fixed size is the mark of an action potential.","","The muscle action potential travels down the T tubules, not the EPP.","Refractory periods belong to action potentials."],"id":"q59"},{"kc":"kc19","q":"One action potential in a motor neuron produces how many action potentials in each muscle fiber it supplies?","c":["None, unless many neurons fire together","Exactly one","Dozens","It depends on stimulus intensity"],"a":1,"why":"Thanks to the safety margin, transmission is one-to-one. Unlike synapses in the brain, no summation is needed.","no":["That describes CNS synapses, where EPSPs must sum. The NMJ needs no summation.","","One in, one out.","The motor neuron AP is all-or-none, and so is the result."],"id":"q60"},{"kc":"kc20","q":"Tetanus toxin blocks inhibitory interneurons in the spinal cord. What would happen to a withdrawal reflex?","c":["It works normally","Flexors and extensors both contract, so the limb stiffens instead of pulling away","Neither muscle group contracts","Only the extensors contract"],"a":1,"why":"Withdrawal needs the extensors inhibited. Without inhibitory interneurons both groups fire and fight: rigidity.","no":["Reciprocal inhibition depends on the very interneurons that are blocked.","","The excitatory side still works, so the flexors still fire.","The flexors still get excited; the problem is that extensors are not silenced."],"id":"q61"},{"kc":"kc20","q":"What is the name for exciting one muscle while inhibiting the muscle that opposes it?","c":["Recruitment","Reciprocal inhibition","Summation","Accommodation"],"a":1,"why":"Reciprocal inhibition lets the working muscle move the joint without fighting its opponent.","no":["Recruitment is activating more motor units or receptors.","","Summation is potentials or twitches adding together.","Accommodation is the lens focusing for near vision."],"id":"q62"},{"kc":"kc20","q":"Which kind of neuron quiets the extensor motor neurons during a withdrawal reflex?","c":["Through an excitatory interneuron","Through an inhibitory interneuron","Directly from the sensory neuron, with no interneuron","From the brain through a descending tract"],"a":1,"why":"An inhibitory interneuron quiets the extensor motor neurons so the flexors can pull the limb away.","no":["Excitation goes to the flexors.","","The withdrawal reflex is polysynaptic; interneurons sit between sensory and motor neurons.","The arc closes in the cord before the brain is involved."],"id":"q63"},{"kc":"kc21","q":"A runner steps on glass with the left foot. Which pattern is correct?","c":["Left flexors excited; right flexors excited","Left flexors excited and extensors inhibited; right extensors excited and flexors inhibited","Left extensors excited; right flexors excited","Only the left leg responds"],"a":1,"why":"The injured left leg withdraws. The right leg does the opposite, stiffening to bear the weight.","no":["Both legs flexing drops you to the ground.","","Reversed for both legs.","The crossed extensor reflex fires in the other leg at the same instant."],"id":"q64"},{"kc":"kc21","q":"Why does the crossed extensor reflex need interneurons that cross to the other side of the cord?","c":["To carry the pain signal to the brain","To reach the motor neurons of the opposite leg","To slow the reflex down","To inhibit the injured leg's flexors"],"a":1,"why":"The opposite leg's motor neurons are on the other side of the cord, so the signal must cross to reach them.","no":["Ascending tracts carry pain to the brain; this is a cord circuit.","","Crossing adds a step, but its purpose is reaching the other side.","The injured leg's flexors are excited, not inhibited."],"id":"q65"},{"kc":"kc21","q":"If the reflex simply mirrored itself and both legs flexed when you stepped on a tack, what would happen?","c":["You would withdraw faster","You would collapse, because nothing would support your weight","Nothing different","The pain would stop sooner"],"a":1,"why":"One leg must extend to hold you up. That is the whole point of the crossed extensor reflex.","no":["Speed is not the issue; balance is.","","Both legs flexing is a fall.","Pain is not changed by the opposite leg."],"id":"q66"},{"kc":"kc22","q":"A weightlifter's arm suddenly gives way when the load is far too heavy. Which receptor triggered that protective relaxation?","c":["Muscle spindle, detecting stretch","Golgi tendon organ, detecting tension","Nociceptor in the skin","Hair cell"],"a":1,"why":"The Golgi tendon organ sits in the tendon and senses tension. Dangerous force triggers an inhibitory reflex that relaxes the muscle.","no":["Spindles sense length and drive contraction, not protective relaxation.","","Skin pain receptors are not what senses tendon force.","Hair cells serve hearing and balance."],"id":"q67"},{"kc":"kc22","q":"How many synapses in the spinal cord does the stretch reflex use?","c":["One: sensory neuron directly onto motor neuron","Two, through one interneuron","Many, through the brain","None"],"a":0,"why":"The stretch reflex is monosynaptic: the spindle afferent synapses directly on the motor neuron of the same muscle.","no":["","The withdrawal reflex uses interneurons; the stretch reflex does not need one.","Stretch reflexes close in the cord.","There is one synapse in the cord."],"id":"q68"},{"kc":"kc22","q":"How are muscle spindles and Golgi tendon organs arranged?","c":["Spindles parallel to the muscle fibers; Golgi tendon organs in series, in the tendon","Spindles in the tendon; Golgi tendon organs parallel to the fibers","Both in the tendon","Both in the skin"],"a":0,"why":"Parallel lets spindles feel changes in muscle length. In series lets the tendon organ feel the force passing through the tendon.","no":["","Backwards.","Only the Golgi tendon organ is in the tendon.","Both are proprioceptors in muscle and tendon."],"id":"q69"},{"kc":"kc23","q":"In malignant hyperthermia, a genetic defect makes the SR Ca²⁺ release channels (ryanodine receptors) stay open under some anesthetics. What happens?","c":["Muscles go limp","Ca²⁺ floods the cytosol and muscles contract and stiffen, burning ATP and making heat","The NMJ stops releasing ACh","The A band shortens"],"a":1,"why":"Uncontrolled Ca²⁺ release keeps cross bridges cycling. That uses huge amounts of ATP and releases heat: rigidity and fever.","no":["Too much Ca²⁺ gives contraction, not limpness.","","The defect is inside the muscle fiber, not at the nerve.","The A band never shortens."],"id":"q70"},{"kc":"kc23","q":"What do the T tubules do?","c":["Store Ca²⁺","Carry the action potential deep into the fiber so the SR releases Ca²⁺ throughout it","Hold the thick filaments in place","Pump Ca²⁺ back after contraction"],"a":1,"why":"T tubules are tunnels of surface membrane that bring the action potential to the SR deep inside the fiber.","no":["The SR stores Ca²⁺.","","Thick filaments are anchored at the M line.","The SERCA pump in the SR membrane does that."],"id":"q71"},{"kc":"kc23","q":"Once a motor neuron stops firing, what lets the muscle relax?","c":["Ca²⁺ leaks out of the cell","The SERCA pump uses ATP to put Ca²⁺ back into the SR, and tropomyosin covers the binding sites again","The thick filaments shorten","ACh stays bound to the receptors"],"a":1,"why":"With Ca²⁺ pumped back into storage, troponin lets go and tropomyosin slides back over the sites. Note that relaxation also costs ATP.","no":["Ca²⁺ goes back into the SR, not out of the cell.","","Filaments never shorten.","Acetylcholinesterase clears ACh; lingering ACh would keep the fiber firing."],"id":"q72"},{"kc":"kc23","q":"An isolated skeletal muscle in a dish with no Ca²⁺ in the bathing fluid still contracts when stimulated, at least for a while. Why?","c":["Its Ca²⁺ comes from the sarcoplasmic reticulum inside the fiber","It uses Na⁺ instead","It does not need Ca²⁺ at all","Its T tubules make Ca²⁺"],"a":0,"why":"Skeletal muscle draws its trigger Ca²⁺ from the internal SR store.","no":["","Na⁺ carries the action potential; it does not trigger the cross bridges.","No Ca²⁺, no contraction.","T tubules carry the signal; they do not make Ca²⁺."],"id":"q73"},{"kc":"kc24","q":"At rest, what keeps myosin heads from binding actin?","c":["Troponin covers the binding sites","Tropomyosin lies over the binding sites, held in place by troponin","ATP glues the heads to the thick filament","The Z lines block them"],"a":1,"why":"Tropomyosin is the cover; troponin is the latch that holds it until Ca²⁺ arrives.","no":["Troponin holds tropomyosin; tropomyosin is the cover.","","ATP detaches heads and re-cocks them; it does not glue them.","Z lines anchor thin filaments at the sarcomere ends."],"id":"q74"},{"kc":"kc24","q":"Suppose troponin could not bind Ca²⁺. A motor neuron fires and the SR releases Ca²⁺. What happens?","c":["Normal contraction","No contraction: tropomyosin stays over the binding sites","The muscle locks in rigor","The SR stops releasing Ca²⁺"],"a":1,"why":"Troponin is the calcium sensor. If it cannot bind Ca²⁺, it never moves tropomyosin, and myosin cannot attach.","no":["Ca²⁺ needs troponin to act.","","Rigor is heads stuck ON actin; here they never get on.","The SR still releases; the signal just cannot be read."],"id":"q75"},{"kc":"kc24","q":"Which order is correct?","c":["Ca²⁺ binds tropomyosin, tropomyosin moves troponin, sites open","Ca²⁺ binds troponin, troponin moves tropomyosin, myosin binding sites on actin open","Ca²⁺ binds myosin, myosin moves actin","Ca²⁺ binds actin directly"],"a":1,"why":"TropoNIN receives the Ca²⁺; tropoMYOSIN moves out of the way.","no":["The reversed-order trap.","","Ca²⁺ does not bind myosin in skeletal muscle.","Ca²⁺ works through the troponin-tropomyosin chain."],"id":"q76"},{"kc":"kc25","q":"Which step of the cross-bridge cycle needs ATP to BIND (not to be split)?","c":["The power stroke","Detaching the myosin head from actin","Releasing Ca²⁺ from the SR","Cocking the head"],"a":1,"why":"ATP binding to myosin lowers its grip on actin, and the head lets go.","no":["Pi release triggers the power stroke.","","The SR releases Ca²⁺ through voltage-triggered channels.","Cocking comes from SPLITTING ATP."],"id":"q77"},{"kc":"kc25","q":"What does splitting ATP (hydrolysis) do for the myosin head?","c":["Detaches it from actin","Re-cocks it, storing energy for the next power stroke","Triggers the power stroke","Pumps Ca²⁺"],"a":1,"why":"Hydrolysis re-cocks the head into its high-energy position, holding on to ADP and Pi.","no":["Binding ATP detaches the head.","","Pi release triggers the stroke.","SERCA pumps Ca²⁺ using its own ATP."],"id":"q78"},{"kc":"kc25","q":"Rigor mortis makes muscles stiff a few hours after death, but it passes after a day or two. Why does it end?","c":["ATP returns","The muscle proteins break down, so the stuck cross bridges come apart","Ca²⁺ is pumped back into the SR","Tropomyosin covers the sites again"],"a":1,"why":"Rigor is stuck cross bridges with no ATP. It ends as enzymes break down the proteins, not because ATP comes back.","no":["A dead cell makes no ATP.","","The pump needs ATP, and there is none.","Covering the sites would not detach heads already bound."],"id":"q79"},{"kc":"kc25","q":"Which event in the cycle actually generates force?","c":["ATP binding","ATP hydrolysis","The power stroke, triggered by Pi release","ADP binding to actin"],"a":2,"why":"The power stroke pulls the thin filament toward the center of the sarcomere. Pi release sets it off.","no":["ATP binding detaches.","Hydrolysis re-cocks and stores energy.","","ADP leaves myosin after the stroke; it does not bind actin."],"id":"q80"},{"kc":"kc26","q":"During contraction, which two regions of the sarcomere get narrower?","c":["A band and H zone","I band and H zone","A band and I band","Only the A band"],"a":1,"why":"Thin filaments slide inward, shrinking the I band (thin only) and the H zone (thick only).","no":["The A band stays the same.","","The A band stays the same.","The A band never changes."],"id":"q81"},{"kc":"kc26","q":"What happens to the Z lines during contraction?","c":["They move farther apart","They move closer together","They disappear","They stay where they are while the A band shrinks"],"a":1,"why":"The thin filaments anchored to the Z lines slide inward, pulling the Z lines toward each other. That is how the sarcomere shortens.","no":["That would be lengthening.","","Z lines remain the sarcomere's boundaries.","The A band does not shrink; the Z lines move."],"id":"q82"},{"kc":"kc26","q":"Why does the A band keep its width during contraction?","c":["It is anchored to the bone","Its width is the length of the thick filaments, and thick filaments do not shorten","Ca²⁺ holds it in place","It is made of tropomyosin"],"a":1,"why":"The A band is the thick filament. Filaments slide; they do not shrink.","no":["Sarcomeres are inside fibers; bone anchoring is irrelevant.","","Ca²⁺ turns cross bridges on; it does not set band widths.","Tropomyosin is part of the thin filament."],"id":"q83"},{"kc":"kc27","q":"Holding a plank position for 60 seconds is mostly which type of contraction?","c":["Isotonic concentric","Isotonic eccentric","Isometric","No contraction, since nothing moves"],"a":2,"why":"Your muscles make tension, but their length and the body's position stay the same: isometric.","no":["Concentric means shortening while moving a load.","Eccentric means lengthening under load.","","Tension is being produced, which is a contraction."],"id":"q84"},{"kc":"kc27","q":"Slowly lowering a heavy box to the floor works the biceps how?","c":["Isometric","Isotonic concentric (shortening)","Isotonic eccentric (lengthening under load)","Not at all; gravity does it"],"a":2,"why":"The biceps stays active while it lengthens, controlling the descent. That is eccentric, the kind that makes you sore.","no":["The elbow angle changes, so length changes.","The biceps is getting longer, not shorter.","","Without the biceps the box would drop."],"id":"q85"},{"kc":"kc27","q":"The name \"isometric\" means:","c":["Same tension","Same length","Same speed","Same load"],"a":1,"why":"Iso means same, metric means length. The name tells you what stays the same.","no":["Same tension is isotonic.","","Neither name is about speed.","Neither name is about load."],"id":"q86"},{"kc":"kc28","q":"A muscle is already very short (over-contracted) when stimulated. Why does it also make less tension?","c":["It runs out of ATP","Thin filaments overlap each other and thick filaments hit the Z lines, so cross bridges interfere","Its nerve is damaged","Ca²⁺ cannot be released"],"a":1,"why":"Too short is as bad as too long: crowded filaments get in each other's way, so fewer working cross bridges form.","no":["ATP is fine; geometry is the problem.","","The nerve is normal in this story.","Ca²⁺ release is normal."],"id":"q87"},{"kc":"kc28","q":"At what length does a muscle produce the most tension?","c":["When stretched as far as possible","At its optimal length, where thick and thin filaments overlap so the most cross bridges form","When fully shortened","Tension does not depend on length"],"a":1,"why":"Maximum overlap gives the maximum number of cross bridges.","no":["Overstretched means little overlap.","","Fully shortened means crowding.","Length-tension shows it clearly does."],"id":"q88"},{"kc":"kc28","q":"A patient's muscle is weak because it is held stretched far past its optimal length. Nerves and ATP are normal. What is the cause?","c":["Too few action potentials","Too little overlap between thick and thin filaments, so fewer cross bridges form","Too much Ca²⁺","Fiber type switching"],"a":1,"why":"Length-tension is geometry. When overlap shrinks, so does the number of heads that can grab actin.","no":["The nerves are normal in this story.","","Ca²⁺ is not the issue.","Fiber type does not change with position."],"id":"q89"},{"kc":"kc29","q":"Chicken leg (dark meat) is rich in myoglobin and mitochondria; breast (white meat) is not. Dark meat is mostly which fiber type?","c":["Fast-glycolytic (Type IIx)","Slow-oxidative (Type I)","Smooth muscle","Cardiac muscle"],"a":1,"why":"Myoglobin is red and stores O₂, and mitochondria make ATP aerobically: the slow-oxidative profile.","no":["Fast-glycolytic fibers are pale, with few mitochondria and little myoglobin.","","Leg meat is skeletal muscle.","Leg meat is skeletal muscle."],"id":"q90"},{"kc":"kc29","q":"Which fiber type fatigues fastest, and why?","c":["Slow-oxidative, because it makes the least force","Fast-glycolytic, because it relies on glycolysis, which uses up glycogen fast and makes little ATP per glucose","Slow-oxidative, because it has too many mitochondria","All fatigue at the same rate"],"a":1,"why":"Glycolysis is quick but inefficient and runs through stored glycogen fast. Few mitochondria means little aerobic backup.","no":["Lower force does not mean faster fatigue; slow-oxidative is the most fatigue resistant.","","Mitochondria are why slow fibers last.","Fatigue resistance is a defining difference between types."],"id":"q91"},{"kc":"kc29","q":"The muscles that hold your posture all day are mostly:","c":["Fast-glycolytic","Slow-oxidative","Fast-glycolytic in the morning, slow-oxidative at night","Smooth muscle"],"a":1,"why":"Posture needs low force for hours: the job of fatigue-resistant slow-oxidative fibers.","no":["They would fatigue within minutes.","","Fiber type does not switch daily.","Postural muscles are skeletal."],"id":"q92"}],"cases":[{"kc":["kc7"],"q":"A sober patient walks with a wide, staggering gait, as if drunk. Strength tests are normal, and she starts movements without trouble. Which structure is most likely damaged?","c":["Cerebellum","Basal nuclei","Medulla","Ventral roots"],"a":0,"why":"Normal strength and normal starting, but poor coordination during movement: the cerebellum.","no":["","Basal nuclei problems show at rest and at the start of movement.","Medulla damage threatens breathing and heart rate.","Ventral root damage causes weakness."],"title":"The unsteady walk","id":"x1"},{"kc":["kc11","kc22"],"q":"A man cannot feel a pinprick anywhere on his left lower leg. He can move the leg normally, but his left knee-jerk reflex is gone. Which structure is damaged?","c":["Ventral roots to the leg","Dorsal roots from the leg","The cerebellum","The motor cortex"],"a":1,"why":"Lost sensation with normal movement points to the dorsal (sensory) roots. The knee jerk is gone too, because its afferent limb (spindle to cord) runs through the dorsal root.","no":["Ventral root damage would weaken the leg; he moves it normally.","","The cerebellum does not carry sensation.","Motor cortex damage would weaken movement, not erase sensation."],"title":"Numb but strong","id":"x2"},{"kc":["kc10"],"q":"A patient is always thirsty, eats far more than usual, and her body temperature swings from day to day. Scans show a small lesion. Where?","c":["Thalamus","Hypothalamus","Cerebellum","Hippocampus"],"a":1,"why":"Thirst, hunger and temperature are homeostatic jobs of the hypothalamus.","no":["The thalamus relays sensation; the name-alike trap.","","The cerebellum coordinates movement.","The hippocampus consolidates memory."],"title":"Thirst and fever","id":"x3"},{"kc":["kc16","kc17"],"q":"A hiker eats the shiny black berries of deadly nightshade (belladonna). An hour later: dry mouth, wide pupils, racing heart, no sweating, and constipation. What is the best explanation?","c":["A drug that blocks nicotinic receptors at the NMJ","A drug that blocks muscarinic receptors, removing parasympathetic effects and sympathetic sweating","A drug that mimics norepinephrine at every synapse","A drug that blocks Ca²⁺ release from the SR"],"a":1,"why":"Deadly nightshade contains atropine, a muscarinic blocker. Lose the parasympathetic brake: fast heart, wide pupils, dry mouth, slow gut. Sweating stops too, because sweat glands use muscarinic receptors.","no":["NMJ blockade would cause paralysis.","","Norepinephrine does not run sweat glands, so sweating would not stop.","That would affect skeletal muscle contraction only."],"title":"The wild berries","id":"x4"},{"kc":["kc18"],"q":"Two days after eating home-canned green beans, a patient has drooping eyelids, double vision, and weakness spreading down the body, then trouble breathing. Sensation is normal. What is happening at the NMJ?","c":["ACh is not being released","ACh is never broken down","The SR is releasing too much Ca²⁺","The dorsal roots are blocked"],"a":0,"why":"Classic botulism: the toxin destroys SNARE proteins, vesicles cannot fuse, and no ACh is released. Flaccid paralysis with normal sensation.","no":["","Unbroken ACh would cause twitching and spasm first.","That gives rigidity, not limp weakness.","Sensation is normal, so the sensory side is fine."],"title":"Home-canned beans","id":"x5"},{"kc":["kc19"],"q":"A young woman's eyelids droop by late afternoon and her arms tire when she brushes her hair, but she is stronger after resting. What is the most likely problem?","c":["Too few working ACh receptors at the end plate","A cerebellar tumor","Too much dopamine","Damaged dorsal roots"],"a":0,"why":"Myasthenia gravis: antibodies remove end-plate receptors. The end-plate potential's safety margin is thin, so weakness grows with repeated use and improves with rest.","no":["","Cerebellar damage causes incoordination, not fatigable weakness.","Dopamine affects the basal nuclei, not the NMJ.","Dorsal roots carry sensation."],"title":"Worse by evening","id":"x6"},{"kc":["kc23"],"q":"During surgery under general anesthesia, a patient's jaw becomes rigid, his muscles stiffen, and his temperature rises fast. Which mechanism has gone wrong?","c":["The SR Ca²⁺ release channels are stuck open","No ACh is being released","The basal nuclei have lost dopamine","The thalamus has stopped relaying"],"a":0,"why":"Malignant hyperthermia: Ca²⁺ pours out of the SR, cross bridges cycle nonstop, and ATP use produces heat.","no":["","No ACh would mean limp muscles.","Dopamine loss takes years and gives tremor, not sudden rigidity and fever.","The thalamus does not control muscle contraction."],"title":"Rigid under anesthesia","id":"x7"},{"kc":["kc8"],"q":"A retired teacher's handwriting has become tiny, his face shows little expression, and he struggles to take the first step through a doorway. His hand trembles when it rests in his lap. Which structure is failing?","c":["Cerebellum","Basal nuclei","Hippocampus","Medulla"],"a":1,"why":"Trouble starting movement plus a resting tremor: the basal nuclei, from lost dopamine (Parkinson's).","no":["Cerebellar problems appear during movement, not at rest.","","The hippocampus is memory.","The medulla runs vital centers."],"title":"The first step","id":"x8"},{"kc":["kc11"],"q":"A child who had polio years ago has thin, weak leg muscles but feels touch and temperature on the legs normally. Which cells were destroyed?","c":["Dorsal root ganglion cells","Motor neurons in the ventral horn","Oligodendrocytes","Thalamic neurons"],"a":1,"why":"Polio kills motor neurons in the ventral horn. Muscles lose their nerve supply and waste; sensation runs through intact dorsal roots.","no":["Those are sensory cell bodies, and sensation is normal.","","Oligodendrocytes make CNS myelin.","Thalamic damage would remove feeling."],"title":"Weak but feeling","id":"x9"},{"kc":["kc9"],"q":"After a severe brain infection, a man can repeat a phone number right away and describes his wedding 30 years ago in detail. Every morning he asks his nurse's name again. Which structure is most likely damaged?","c":["Cerebellum","Hippocampus","Thalamus","Medulla"],"a":1,"why":"Short-term memory works, old memories work, new long-term memories do not form: consolidation has failed, a hippocampal job.","no":["The cerebellum coordinates movement.","","The thalamus relays sensation.","The medulla runs vital centers."],"title":"The nurse's name","id":"x10"},{"kc":["kc12"],"q":"A teenager with a complete spinal cord injury at mid-back steps barefoot onto a sharp rock months later. His foot jerks back, but he says he felt nothing. Where did the reflex close, and why no feeling?","c":["In the brain; the injury blocks memory of the pain","In the cord below the injury; the ascending tracts to the brain are cut","In the foot muscle itself","In the thalamus"],"a":1,"why":"The withdrawal arc is complete in the lower cord. The cable carrying the signal up to the brain is severed, so nothing is felt.","no":["The brain cannot be in the loop; the cord is cut above.","","Muscles do not sense the rock; receptors and neurons do.","The signal never reaches the thalamus."],"title":"The sharp rock","id":"x11"},{"kc":["kc29"],"q":"A coach tests two athletes. Athlete A has thighs full of fibers with many mitochondria and lots of myoglobin. Athlete B's fibers are pale with few mitochondria. Which event suits each best?","c":["A: shot put; B: marathon","A: marathon; B: 100-meter sprint","Both: marathon","Both: sprint"],"a":1,"why":"Many mitochondria and myoglobin mean slow-oxidative endurance fibers. Pale, few-mitochondria fibers are fast-glycolytic: powerful but quick to tire.","no":["Reversed.","","B's fibers would tire fast in a marathon.","A's fibers are built for endurance."],"title":"The two athletes","id":"x12"}],"order":[{"kc":["kc18"],"title":"The neuromuscular junction","prompt":"Put the NMJ in order, from the motor neuron to the muscle.","steps":["Action potential arrives at the motor axon terminal","Voltage-gated Ca²⁺ channels open","Ca²⁺ enters the terminal","Vesicles fuse with the membrane (SNARE proteins)","ACh is released into the cleft","ACh binds nicotinic receptors on the end plate","End-plate potential","Muscle action potential","Acetylcholinesterase breaks down ACh"],"why":"Ca²⁺ is the release trigger, and the end-plate potential always reaches threshold. Acetylcholinesterase ends the signal.","id":"o1"},{"kc":["kc23","kc24"],"title":"From muscle action potential to cross bridge","prompt":"Put excitation-contraction coupling in order.","steps":["Action potential travels along the sarcolemma","It dives down the T tubules","Voltage sensors open the SR Ca²⁺ release channels","Ca²⁺ floods the cytosol","Ca²⁺ binds troponin","Troponin moves tropomyosin off the binding sites","Myosin heads bind actin","Power stroke pulls the thin filaments inward"],"why":"Electrical signal on the surface, Ca²⁺ from the SR inside, then the troponin-tropomyosin switch lets the cross bridges work.","id":"o2"},{"kc":["kc25"],"title":"The cross-bridge cycle","prompt":"Start with a myosin head attached to actin. Put the cycle in order.","steps":["ATP binds myosin and the head detaches from actin","ATP is split and the head re-cocks (ADP and Pi stay on)","The cocked head binds actin","Pi is released and the power stroke pulls the thin filament","ADP is released; the head is attached and ready for a new ATP"],"why":"ATP binding detaches, splitting re-cocks, Pi release powers the stroke. No ATP means no detaching: rigor.","id":"o3"},{"kc":["kc12","kc11"],"title":"The withdrawal reflex arc","prompt":"You touch a hot stove. Put the reflex in order.","steps":["Pain receptor in the skin is stimulated","Afferent (sensory) neuron carries the signal","Signal enters the cord through the dorsal root","Interneuron in the gray matter","Efferent (motor) neuron fires","Signal leaves through the ventral root","Flexor muscle contracts and the hand pulls away"],"why":"Receptor, afferent neuron, integrating center, efferent neuron, effector. Dorsal in, ventral out, and the brain hears about it afterward.","id":"o4"},{"kc":["kc14"],"title":"How a hair cell hears","prompt":"Put hearing transduction in order.","steps":["Sound vibrates the basilar membrane","Stereocilia bend toward the tallest one","Tip links stretch","Mechanically gated cation channels open","K⁺ flows in and the hair cell depolarizes (graded)","The hair cell releases more transmitter","The afferent neuron fires more action potentials"],"why":"Bending becomes a graded receptor potential in the hair cell; the afferent neuron is the one that fires action potentials.","id":"o5"},{"kc":["kc15"],"title":"A sympathetic signal to the heart","prompt":"Put the pathway in order, from the cord to the heart.","steps":["Preganglionic neuron leaves the thoracolumbar cord","It releases ACh at the ganglion","ACh binds nicotinic receptors on the postganglionic neuron","Postganglionic neuron releases norepinephrine at the heart","Norepinephrine binds adrenergic (beta-1) receptors","Heart rate and force increase"],"why":"ACh on nicotinic at every ganglion, then norepinephrine on adrenergic at most sympathetic targets.","id":"o6"},{"kc":["kc5"],"title":"Protecting the brain","prompt":"Put the brain's protection in order, from outside in.","steps":["Skull","Dura mater","Arachnoid mater","CSF in the subarachnoid space","Pia mater","Blood-brain barrier at the capillaries"],"why":"Bone, three meninges with the fluid cushion between arachnoid and pia, then the chemical barrier at the capillaries.","id":"o7"},{"kc":["kc10","kc6","kc3"],"title":"From a touch to feeling it","prompt":"Someone taps your hand. Put the path to conscious feeling in order.","steps":["Touch receptor in the skin","Afferent neuron through the dorsal root","Ascending tract in the white matter of the cord","Relay in the thalamus","Somatosensory cortex in the parietal lobe"],"why":"Up the cable, through the relay station, onto the body map. Cut any link and the touch is not felt.","id":"o8"},{"kc":["kc9"],"title":"Making a memory","prompt":"Put memory formation in order.","steps":["Sensory input","Short-term memory","Consolidation by the hippocampus","Long-term memory"],"why":"The hippocampus moves material from short-term into long-term storage. Damage it and the chain breaks at that step.","id":"o9"}],"tables":[{"kc":["kc1","kc2"],"title":"Glial cells and their jobs","head":["Cell","Where","Job"],"rows":[["Oligodendrocyte","CNS","Myelin, many axons per cell"],["Schwann cell","PNS","Myelin, one segment per cell"],["Astrocyte","CNS","Induces the blood-brain barrier"],["Ependymal cell","CNS","Lines ventricles, helps make CSF"],["Microglia","CNS","Immune defense"]],"id":"t1"},{"kc":["kc3"],"title":"Gray and white matter","head":["","Brain","Spinal cord"],"rows":[["Gray matter","Outside (cortex)","Inside (butterfly)"],["White matter","Deep, under the cortex","Outside, around the gray"]],"id":"t2"},{"kc":["kc15","kc16"],"title":"The autonomic transmitter-receptor table","head":["Fiber","Transmitter","Receptor"],"rows":[["All preganglionic (both divisions)","Acetylcholine","Nicotinic"],["Parasympathetic postganglionic","Acetylcholine","Muscarinic"],["Sympathetic postganglionic (most)","Norepinephrine","Adrenergic"],["Sympathetic to sweat glands","Acetylcholine","Muscarinic"],["Somatic motor neuron (NMJ)","Acetylcholine","Nicotinic"]],"id":"t3"},{"kc":["kc17"],"title":"Dual innervation","head":["Organ","Sympathetic","Parasympathetic"],"rows":[["Heart rate","Increases","Decreases"],["Airways","Dilate","Constrict"],["Pupil","Dilates","Constricts"],["Digestive motility","Decreases","Increases"],["Saliva","Small and thick","Copious and watery"],["Bladder wall","Relaxes","Contracts"]],"id":"t4"},{"kc":["kc11"],"title":"Dorsal and ventral roots","head":["Root","Direction","Carries"],"rows":[["Dorsal","Into the cord","Afferent (sensory)"],["Ventral","Out of the cord","Efferent (motor)"]],"id":"t5"},{"kc":["kc9"],"title":"After hippocampal damage","head":["Memory","Status"],"rows":[["Short-term memory","Intact"],["Old long-term memories","Intact"],["New long-term memories","Cannot form"]],"id":"t6"},{"kc":["kc7","kc8"],"title":"Cerebellum or basal nuclei?","head":["","Cerebellum","Basal nuclei"],"rows":[["When symptoms show","During movement","At rest and at the start"],["Tremor","Intention tremor","Resting tremor"],["Classic cause","Alcohol, stroke, tumor","Parkinson's, Huntington's"]],"id":"t7"},{"kc":["kc22"],"title":"Muscle spindle vs Golgi tendon organ","head":["","Muscle spindle","Golgi tendon organ"],"rows":[["Location","Parallel to fibers","In series, in the tendon"],["Detects","Length (stretch)","Tension (force)"],["Reflex","Monosynaptic stretch reflex","Polysynaptic inhibitory reflex"]],"id":"t8"},{"kc":["kc20","kc21"],"title":"Stepping on a tack","head":["Leg","Flexors","Extensors"],"rows":[["Injured leg","Excited","Inhibited"],["Opposite leg","Inhibited","Excited"]],"id":"t9"},{"kc":["kc26"],"title":"Bands during contraction","head":["Band or zone","During contraction"],"rows":[["A band","Stays the same"],["I band","Narrows"],["H zone","Narrows"],["Z lines","Move closer together"]],"id":"t10"},{"kc":["kc27"],"title":"Isometric vs isotonic","head":["","Isometric","Isotonic"],"rows":[["Length","Stays the same","Changes"],["Tension","Rises","Stays the same"],["Load","Does not move","Moves"]],"id":"t11"},{"kc":["kc29"],"title":"Fiber types","head":["","Slow-oxidative (Type I)","Fast-glycolytic (Type IIx)"],"rows":[["ATP pathway","Aerobic","Glycolysis"],["Mitochondria","Many","Few"],["Fatigue","Resists fatigue","Fatigues fast"],["Force","Lower","Higher"],["Example","Posture, marathon","Sprint, heavy lift"]],"id":"t12"}],"explain":[{"kc":["kc2"],"prompt":"A drug kills brain tumor cells in a dish but does nothing when given to patients by vein. Explain why.","model":"Brain capillaries are sealed by tight junctions that astrocyte foot processes induce: the blood-brain barrier. A molecule must pass through the capillary cells, so lipid-soluble substances get in but most water-soluble drugs do not. In the dish there was no barrier.","points":["Names the blood-brain barrier","Astrocytes induce it","Tight junctions between capillary cells","Lipid-soluble passes, water-soluble is blocked"],"id":"e1"},{"kc":["kc13"],"prompt":"A friend says, \"A harder pinch makes a bigger nerve signal.\" Correct them like a physiologist.","model":"Each action potential is all-or-none, so it cannot get bigger. A harder pinch makes a bigger graded receptor potential, which makes the neuron fire more action potentials per second (frequency coding), and it activates more receptors (recruitment). The brain reads the pattern as stronger.","points":["Action potentials are all-or-none (same size)","Receptor potential is graded and grows","Higher firing frequency","More receptors recruited"],"id":"e2"},{"kc":["kc14"],"prompt":"Explain how a sound vibration becomes a signal in the auditory nerve.","model":"Sound moves the basilar membrane and the stereocilia bend toward the tallest one. Tip links stretch and pull open mechanically gated cation channels; K⁺ enters and the hair cell depolarizes, a graded receptor potential. The hair cell releases more transmitter, and the afferent neuron fires more action potentials.","points":["Stereocilia bend toward the tallest","Tip links open mechanically gated channels","Cations (K⁺) in, graded depolarization","Hair cell releases transmitter","Afferent neuron fires the action potentials"],"id":"e3"},{"kc":["kc18","kc19"],"prompt":"Explain why the neuromuscular junction almost never fails, and how myasthenia gravis makes it fail.","model":"The motor neuron releases far more ACh than needed, so the end-plate potential, though graded, is far above threshold: a big safety margin, and one action potential in the neuron gives one in the fiber. In myasthenia gravis, antibodies destroy end-plate receptors, the end-plate potential shrinks, and with repeated firing some fall below threshold: weakness that worsens with use.","points":["End-plate potential is graded","Big safety margin above threshold","Myasthenia destroys ACh receptors","Smaller EPP can fall below threshold","Weakness gets worse with repeated use"],"id":"e4"},{"kc":["kc23","kc24"],"prompt":"Explain what Ca²⁺ does after it is released inside a skeletal muscle fiber, and how the fiber relaxes.","model":"Ca²⁺ from the sarcoplasmic reticulum binds troponin. Troponin shifts tropomyosin off the myosin binding sites on actin, so cross bridges form and cycle. To relax, the SERCA pump uses ATP to put Ca²⁺ back into the SR; troponin lets go and tropomyosin covers the sites again.","points":["Ca²⁺ comes from the SR","Binds troponin","Tropomyosin moves off the binding sites","Cross bridges form","SERCA pumps Ca²⁺ back using ATP"],"id":"e5"},{"kc":["kc25"],"prompt":"Explain why a muscle needs ATP to RELAX, not just to contract.","model":"In the cross-bridge cycle, ATP binding is what makes the myosin head let go of actin. Without ATP the heads stay attached, which is rigor mortis. ATP also powers the SERCA pump that returns Ca²⁺ to the SR so tropomyosin can cover the binding sites again.","points":["ATP binding detaches myosin from actin","No ATP means heads stay stuck (rigor)","SERCA uses ATP to pump Ca²⁺ back"],"id":"e6"},{"kc":["kc28"],"prompt":"A patient's muscle is weak while held overstretched, though its nerve and ATP supply are normal. Explain the weakness.","model":"Tension depends on how many cross bridges can form, and that depends on overlap between thick and thin filaments. Overstretched, the filaments barely overlap, so few myosin heads can reach actin and tension falls. It is geometry, not a nerve or fuel problem.","points":["Tension depends on the number of cross bridges","Overstretching reduces thick-thin overlap","Fewer heads reach actin","Not a nerve or ATP problem"],"id":"e7"},{"kc":["kc7","kc8"],"prompt":"Compare how a cerebellar lesion and Parkinson's disease look in a patient.","model":"Cerebellar damage shows during movement: overshoot, wobble near the target (intention tremor), unsteady gait, with normal strength and normal starting. Parkinson's, a basal nuclei disorder from lost dopamine, shows at rest and at the start: a resting tremor that fades with purposeful movement, and trouble starting movements.","points":["Cerebellum: problems during movement","Intention tremor, overshoot","Basal nuclei: resting tremor","Trouble starting movement","Parkinson's = dopamine loss"],"id":"e8"},{"kc":["kc4"],"prompt":"Why can a person survive losing a large part of the cerebral cortex but not a small part of the medulla?","model":"The medulla (with the pons) holds the vital centers that set breathing rhythm, heart rate and blood pressure. Lose them and the body cannot breathe or circulate blood on its own. Cortical areas handle thinking and perception, which are not needed to stay alive.","points":["Medulla holds vital centers","Breathing, heart rate, blood pressure","Cortex is not needed for survival"],"id":"e9"},{"kc":["kc16"],"prompt":"Explain why the sympathetic fibers to sweat glands are called \"the exception.\"","model":"Most sympathetic postganglionic fibers release norepinephrine onto adrenergic receptors. The fibers to sweat glands are anatomically sympathetic, from the thoracolumbar cord and active in fight or flight, but they release acetylcholine onto muscarinic receptors. That is why Botox and atropine reduce sweating.","points":["Usual rule: NE on adrenergic","Sweat glands: ACh on muscarinic","Still sympathetic by anatomy"],"id":"e10"},{"kc":["kc21","kc20"],"prompt":"Someone steps on a nail. Describe what both legs do, and why each response matters.","model":"Injured leg: the withdrawal reflex excites its flexors and inhibits its extensors (reciprocal inhibition), so the foot lifts off the nail. Opposite leg: the crossed extensor reflex excites its extensors and inhibits its flexors, so it stiffens and holds the body weight. Without it, you would fall.","points":["Injured leg flexors excited","Injured leg extensors inhibited","Opposite leg extensors excited","Opposite leg supports the weight"],"id":"e11"},{"kc":["kc12","kc10"],"prompt":"A man has a complete spinal cord injury at mid-back. Explain why a pinprick on his foot can still make it withdraw, but he cannot feel it.","model":"The withdrawal arc (receptor, afferent neuron, interneuron, motor neuron, muscle) closes in the lower cord, below the injury, so the reflex still runs. Conscious feeling needs the signal to travel up the ascending tracts, through the thalamus, to the cortex, and the injury has cut that cable.","points":["Reflex arc closes in the cord below the injury","Feeling needs ascending tracts to the brain","The injury cuts that cable"],"id":"e12"}],"decks":[]};
